  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "Let a binary operation \\(*\\) on ordered pairs of integers be defined by \\((a,b)*(c,d)=(a-c,b+d).\\) Then, if \\((3,2)*(0,0)\\) and \\((x,y)*(3,2)\\) represent identical pairs, \\(x\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-3\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Apply the stated operation to each expression, keeping the operands in their given order:\n\\[\n(3,2)*(0,0)=(3-0,2+0)=(3,2),\n\\]\n\\[\n(x,y)*(3,2)=(x-3,y+2).\n\\]\nIdentical ordered pairs have equal corresponding entries. Hence\n\\[\nx-3=3,\\qquad y+2=2,\n\\]\nwhich gives \\(x=6\\) and \\(y=0\\). These values indeed make both resulting pairs \\((3,2)\\). Therefore \\(x=6\\), choice E.",
